# G039 — Mail Checker

*v2. See `mail-checker.md` for prose: polling as alarm-clock applied
to external state, inbox as filtered priority stream, read-state as
agent attention, notification callbacks as event-driven alternative
to polling.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Morning Briefing as Mail Check

[morning-briefing @since
    IN ORDER TO
        concurrently drain three executive inboxes filtered to unread-since
        compose a single morning briefing from the matched messages
        and leave every inbox with zero unread after the briefing runs

    concurrent [
        finance:     @fin <- [inbox[check
                                      {owner: "kathryn",
                                       filter: {since: @since,
                                                 unread-only: true}}]]
    |
        engineering: @eng <- [inbox[check
                                      {owner: "jay",
                                       filter: {since: @since,
                                                 unread-only: true}}]]
    |
        editorial:   @ed  <- [inbox[check
                                      {owner: "lena",
                                       filter: {since: @since,
                                                 unread-only: true}}]]
    ]
    join

    @briefing <- [compose
                    {finance: @fin:matched,
                     engineering: @eng:matched,
                     editorial: @ed:matched}]

    @fin, @eng, @ed
        where each has checked is true
        and each has new-remaining is zero
            -> [ui[show-briefing {briefing: @briefing}]]
        otherwise
            -> #briefing-stale (mail arrived during assembly)
]

---

## Verb Surface

[inbox
    check               {owner, filter}          ;; -> {matched, checked, new-remaining}
    mark-read           {owner, uid}
    flag                {owner, uid}
    unflag              {owner, uid}
    on-new              {owner, handler}         ;; event-driven variant
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: three-state attention `where` processed and actionable diverge

[@message
    where is-read is false                       -> #unprocessed (attention debt)
    where is-read is true and is-flagged is false -> #acknowledged
    where is-flagged is true                      -> #actionable (requires future action)
]

Read-state is binary attention. Flagging is prioritised attention.
Together they form a triage protocol for the agent's queue —
unprocessed, acknowledged, actionable.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: filter as `where`-gated pipeline

[inbox[check {owner: @o, filter: @f}]
    IN ORDER TO
        apply progressively cheaper gates first
        so expensive content searches only run on the reduced set

    @o:messages
        where sender matches @f:sender-pattern         ;; cheap string
        where subject matches @f:subject-pattern       ;; cheap string
        where since @f:since                            ;; date comparison
        where body contains @f:body-needle              ;; moderate scan
        where unread-only implies is-read is false
    IN ORDER TO
        cheapest gate first is the standard staged-filter pattern —
        reduce the set before doing substring searches over bodies
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @yesterday <- [clock[now]] - 86400000
    -> [inbox[check
                {owner: "kathryn",
                 filter: {since: @yesterday,
                           unread-only: true,
                           subject-pattern: "budget"}}]]
    ;; -> {matched: [msg-ids], checked: true, new-remaining: 0}

    [inbox[mark-read {owner: "kathryn", uid: @msg-id}]]
    -> [inbox[check
                {owner: "kathryn",
                 filter: {unread-only: true}}]]
    ;; -> {matched: [...], checked: true, new-remaining: ...}
]
